01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:42.120
<v Mike>Here's the interesting thing and you're getting to what my favorite part of the game is that

01:03:42.120 --> 01:03:46.020
<v Mike>if we think about the Grand Theft Autos or even the L.A.

01:03:46.060 --> 01:03:50.380
<v Mike>Noire of all that stuff, those

01:03:50.380 --> 01:03:55.340
<v Mike>games are so fun not because of the actual story mode, but because of the sandbox environment

01:03:55.340 --> 01:03:58.160
<v Mike>you're put in and how you choose to interact with things.

01:03:58.160 --> 01:04:05.060
<v Mike>And while this game isn't on that scale by any means, there is so much fun that you can

01:04:05.060 --> 01:04:08.540
<v Sean>have in this game by acting like a jackass.

01:04:08.820 --> 01:04:17.200
<v Mike>Oh, yeah. You can get the gun, select shoot, and then self, and the game will be like, oh, wait,

01:04:17.200 --> 01:04:18.300
<v Sean>go out with a bang.

01:04:20.980 --> 01:04:22.540
<v Sean>I did appreciate that.

01:04:22.540 --> 01:04:24.880
<v Sean>I did appreciate that you could shoot whoever you want.

01:04:25.220 --> 01:04:27.460
<v Sean>You would, like, that is the end state.

01:04:27.680 --> 01:04:35.660
<v Sean>I did like that I thought I got around the money thing by starting a car with a key I found,

01:04:35.680 --> 01:04:37.280
<v Sean>and then you immediately just blow up.

01:04:37.640 --> 01:04:43.380
<v Sean>Yeah. And it's not even just like a bullshit, like, wow, how was I supposed to know that?

01:04:43.460 --> 01:04:47.460
<v Sean>Like, if you talk to somebody, or maybe if you read a note or something.

01:04:47.540 --> 01:04:48.520
<v Joe>No, it's the woman.

01:04:48.720 --> 01:04:52.500
<v Sean>Oh, it's the woman. Yeah, she's just like, yeah, yeah, I read that to blow.

01:04:52.720 --> 01:04:53.700
<v Sean>And it's like, oh, okay.

01:04:54.580 --> 01:05:02.980
<v Sean>Yeah. So I do appreciate the level of interaction and all the fun fail states that you got here.

01:05:02.980 --> 01:05:07.840
<v Mike>Yeah. Another fun thing is, like, forget fail states.

01:05:07.980 --> 01:05:11.800
<v Mike>You can also, like, soft lock yourself out of the game by doing really stupid things like

01:05:11.800 --> 01:05:16.680
<v Mike>flushing toilets, flushing your inventory stuff down the toilet.

01:05:16.860 --> 01:05:21.320
<v Mike>Like, literally watching, saying goodbye to the game as you watch it swirl down.

01:05:21.880 --> 01:05:23.400
<v Mike>There's no animation for it, of course.

01:05:23.560 --> 01:05:25.260
<v Mike>The game is kind of lacking that.

01:05:25.420 --> 01:05:28.520
<v Mike>It's all still screens instead of animated screens.

01:05:29.620 --> 01:05:33.420
<v Mike>But then there's also, like, the game is kind of snarky.

01:05:33.540 --> 01:05:34.960
<v Mike>I think you referenced that, Sean.

01:05:35.100 --> 01:05:38.860
<v Mike>But, like, if you're being stupid, it'll imply so.

01:05:39.080 --> 01:05:42.360
<v Mike>But, like, you can use the speak command on that same toilet.

01:05:42.400 --> 01:05:44.920
<v Mike>And the game will tell you, like, you're talking to a toilet.

01:05:45.380 --> 01:05:46.880
<v Sean>Really? I didn't know that.

01:05:47.220 --> 01:05:52.380
<v Sean>Yeah, I didn't either. It's the games that I tried speaking to, and it would just be, like, what are you doing?

01:05:52.600 --> 01:05:57.540
<v Sean>And I thought for a while it was, like, oh, wow, it's actually, maybe it's, if only if

01:05:57.540 --> 01:05:59.240
<v Mike>I talk to that thing it says that.

01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:02.760
<v Sean>But, no. If you talk to inanimate objects, usually it just asks you what you're doing.

01:06:03.680 --> 01:06:08.240
<v Joe>Yeah. Or it says, like, or if you examine them, it says, like, nothing unusual here.

01:06:08.420 --> 01:06:10.880
<v Joe>If you just, like, examine, like, blank spaces or whatever.

01:06:10.880 --> 01:06:15.220
<v Joe>But it is cool that, like, sometimes it does have, like, a little flavor text just to add

01:06:15.220 --> 01:06:17.920
<v Joe>to it. Like, there's a lot to discover in the game.

01:06:18.040 --> 01:06:23.120
<v Joe>Not even just on the way to beating the game, but just exploring the world and seeing what

01:06:23.120 --> 01:06:30.840
<v Mike>you can do. Throughout the game, there are these, I guess we'll call them deja vu moments where there's,

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:33.760
<v Mike>like, a music cue, and I guess it shows you.

01:06:34.260 --> 01:06:36.660
<v Mike>So now it switches POVs.

01:06:37.320 --> 01:06:38.420
<v Mike>But it shows you.

01:06:38.420 --> 01:06:43.060
<v Mike>And it reveals, like, a, you know, a twist of sorts.

01:06:43.660 --> 01:06:44.920
<v Mike>They get grander.

01:06:45.140 --> 01:06:53.640
<v Mike>But that deja vu moment as a repetitive cue also works really well from a, like, not repetition,

01:06:53.800 --> 01:06:55.240
<v Mike>but even just sound perspective.

01:06:55.560 --> 01:06:56.360
<v Sean>How do you mean?

01:06:56.840 --> 01:07:00.920
<v Mike>Like, just, you know, that, like, every time you hear that specific thing, it's like you've

01:07:00.920 --> 01:07:06.900
<v Mike>learned something bigger than just, like, collecting another thing in your inventory or

01:07:08.160 --> 01:07:08.960
<v Mike>another address.

01:07:09.440 --> 01:07:10.620
<v Sean>Yeah, I guess it was.

01:07:10.740 --> 01:07:17.760
<v Sean>I didn't really put together that whatever was happening would be, like, a big moment.

01:07:17.860 --> 01:07:21.460
<v Sean>Because I remember I was just going up some stairs, and I thought I was just getting, like,

01:07:21.520 --> 01:07:24.740
<v Sean>lightheaded. Because it's like, you don't feel so hot.

01:07:25.740 --> 01:07:28.820
<v Sean>You really need to take a break or something.

01:07:29.000 --> 01:07:30.540
<v Mike>And I'm like, I'm just walking up some stairs.

01:07:30.860 --> 01:07:34.140
<v Sean>And then it didn't happen again until I, like, went to an office.

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:38.000
<v Sean>And I guess it was my own office.

01:07:38.020 --> 01:07:39.780
<v Mike>But maybe you don't know that yet.

01:07:40.080 --> 01:07:44.260
<v Sean>And you're sort of getting that deja vu, as you might say.

01:07:44.340 --> 01:07:45.520
<v Mike>So, yeah, I guess I see that.

01:07:45.900 --> 01:07:49.240
<v Joe>Yeah. Or, like, when you look at the picture and you recognize yourself.

01:07:49.660 --> 01:07:50.760
<v Mike>That I didn't see.

01:07:50.760 --> 01:07:55.380
<v Joe>Oh, yeah. There's, like, a picture on the wall that is of Bob Oxer.

01:07:55.560 --> 01:07:59.320
<v Joe>It's you. And then that image comes up of your face, and that music plays.

01:07:59.540 --> 01:08:02.840
<v Joe>And it's a little corny, but it is kind of, like, it is kind of cool.

01:08:03.000 --> 01:08:04.240
<v Joe>And it's kind of, like, mysterious.

01:08:04.240 --> 01:08:08.240
<v Joe>And it catches your interest of, like, oh, man, what is going on?

01:08:08.320 --> 01:08:10.460
<v Joe>Like, this is such a creepy situation for this guy to be in.

01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:13.360
<v Joe>You got to just kind of suspend your, like, cringe a little bit.

01:08:13.780 --> 01:08:17.840
<v Mike>And while you guys were talking about whether or not you pulled the items out of the coat,

01:08:17.860 --> 01:08:19.960
<v Mike>the first moment where I...

01:08:20.760 --> 01:08:26.040
<v Mike>I had to actually look at a guide was because the desk in the...

01:08:26.040 --> 01:08:28.780
<v Mike>Above the bar in the office didn't...

01:08:28.780 --> 01:08:29.700
<v Mike>You weren't...

01:08:29.700 --> 01:08:32.040
<v Mike>Because of the point of view, you're facing...

01:08:32.040 --> 01:08:33.500
<v Mike>It didn't read like a desk, did it?

01:08:33.680 --> 01:08:36.380
<v Mike>It didn't... I mean, like, the guy was hunched over it, if I recall.

01:08:36.520 --> 01:08:38.220
<v Mike>Like, there was another one that wasn't.

01:08:38.340 --> 01:08:40.540
<v Mike>But there was another one where a guy's hunched over the desk.

01:08:40.540 --> 01:08:41.480
<v Mike>So, you knew it was a desk.

01:08:41.700 --> 01:08:44.220
<v Mike>But he... The drawers would be on his side.

01:08:44.400 --> 01:08:45.980
<v Sean>So, you would think you have to, like... Yeah, you can't see there's anything to open.

01:08:46.220 --> 01:08:49.760
<v Mike>Right, right. But the only way to progress is to open those drawers.

01:08:50.160 --> 01:08:54.300
<v Mike>And so, I guess I could have just tried spamming commands around the room.

01:08:54.460 --> 01:08:55.940
<v Mike>But I don't have time for that.

01:08:56.140 --> 01:08:57.060
<v Sean>So, that was...

01:08:57.060 --> 01:08:58.260
<v Joe>Yeah, it kind of defeats the purpose of the game.

01:08:58.360 --> 01:08:59.580
<v Joe>You're not even playing the game anymore.

01:08:59.700 --> 01:09:00.540
<v Sean>You're just... I got there.

01:09:00.760 --> 01:09:03.660
<v Sean>That's like, you know, maybe you guys knew you could gamble.

01:09:04.060 --> 01:09:06.240
<v Sean>At least I opened that...

01:09:06.240 --> 01:09:08.720
<v Sean>I opened that desk pretty easily.

01:09:10.200 --> 01:09:11.740
<v Joe>Yeah, I did the same thing as Mike.

01:09:11.840 --> 01:09:13.800
<v Joe>I was like... I had no idea.

01:09:14.080 --> 01:09:16.040
<v Joe>So, I finally looked at a guide and it said, like, open the desk.

01:09:16.920 --> 01:09:18.240
<v Mike>And I was like, oh, yeah.

01:09:18.320 --> 01:09:22.120
<v Joe>And there's no visual indication that there's, like, a drawer involved in this desk.

01:09:22.460 --> 01:09:27.760
<v Mike>Because if you know everything to do in the game via a guide or whatever, it's a pretty short game.

01:09:27.760 --> 01:09:30.300
<v Mike>But it's also, like...

01:09:31.100 --> 01:09:33.540
<v Mike>It's not even short because you're just doing everything.

01:09:33.540 --> 01:09:36.640
<v Mike>You're missing the actual personality of the game.

01:09:36.840 --> 01:09:40.920
<v Mike>I feel like the best part of this game is the vibe.

01:09:41.260 --> 01:09:43.520
<v Mike>Like, just the world that they put you in.

01:09:43.720 --> 01:09:48.940
<v Mike>Not that it is, again, keeping up with modern open worlds or anything like that.

01:09:49.040 --> 01:09:55.760
<v Mike>But for what we've experienced, like, in terms of building out a story and a character and the people you interact with,

01:09:56.360 --> 01:09:59.620
<v Mike>I had more fun just tinkering with things.

01:09:59.620 --> 01:10:01.500
<v Mike>Kind of like what we did in Maniac Mansion, too.

01:10:01.500 --> 01:10:04.260
<v Mike>Where you would just, like, fuck around in the mansion for a little bit.

01:10:04.400 --> 01:10:06.660
<v Mike>You know? Like, yeah. Let's see what's going on over here, right?

01:10:06.760 --> 01:10:09.580
<v Mike>And, like, everybody was, like, following their own scripts.

01:10:09.960 --> 01:10:11.700
<v Mike>That wasn't quite what this game was doing.

01:10:11.700 --> 01:10:14.400
<v Mike>But I was interested in just exploring it.

01:10:14.440 --> 01:10:21.800
<v Mike>So, like, when you got a new address, I wanted to make sure I, like, saw everything around here first before I went to the next location.

01:10:21.800 --> 01:10:30.160
<v Sean>Yeah. That was a priority for me in a lot of games, I think, in my earlier days.

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:33.280
<v Sean>It's just, like, I want a bunch of toys to play with.

01:10:33.380 --> 01:10:37.620
<v Sean>And I think everywhere you go in this game is kind of like a fun house in itself.

01:10:37.820 --> 01:10:40.500
<v Sean>And there are weird ways to interact with stuff.

01:10:40.640 --> 01:10:43.400
<v Sean>So, yeah. This was very much up my alley in that sense.

01:10:43.400 --> 01:10:50.120
<v Mike>Now, is the actual story, though, as strong as the world that you're put in?

01:10:50.260 --> 01:10:51.940
<v Mike>Like, is this amnesia?

01:10:52.180 --> 01:10:55.540
<v Mike>And then, like, I guess some spoilers here.

01:10:55.720 --> 01:11:02.600
<v Mike>But eventually you find out that you're being framed by the mafia for the murder of somebody that they didn't agree with.

01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:07.920
<v Mike>And the doctor that you went to go see was actually not a good doctor at all.

01:11:08.060 --> 01:11:13.440
<v Mike>But the one who injected you with these amnesia drugs and you need to take medicine against them.

01:11:13.620 --> 01:11:15.880
<v Mike>Is that part compelling?

01:11:16.200 --> 01:11:17.820
<v Mike>Does that part, like, hold up?

01:11:18.560 --> 01:11:22.120
<v Joe>I don't know if I were watching a movie or reading a book.

01:11:22.140 --> 01:11:25.380
<v Joe>I don't know if this would be like, oh, my God, what a standout story.

01:11:25.380 --> 01:11:36.560
<v Joe>But because I'm doing it and I'm solving it and I'm playing an NES game that is causing me to experience this story myself, it does stand out to me.

01:11:36.640 --> 01:11:40.660
<v Joe>I mean, as far as stories on the NES, this was one of the more engaging.

01:11:41.560 --> 01:11:44.900
<v Joe>And, you know, there's tropes and there's generic parts.

01:11:45.020 --> 01:11:47.040
<v Joe>But also, like, there's stuff I didn't see coming.

01:11:47.040 --> 01:11:55.160
<v Joe>And I was interested enough to, you know, for it to keep driving me forward and not just like, oh, I want to see what this game can do.

01:11:55.320 --> 01:11:56.260
<v Joe>What is in this area?

01:11:56.440 --> 01:12:00.380
<v Joe>What's it's like? No, I do want to find things to help me solve this mystery.

01:12:00.460 --> 01:12:10.600
<v Joe>And it does give you this satisfaction when you find something that feels like it's going to be like a key part in in the solving of the mystery.

01:12:11.000 --> 01:12:16.600
<v Sean>Yeah, it's funny. I'd actually because I didn't do it how I soft black myself several times.

01:12:16.920 --> 01:12:24.700
<v Sean>But so I didn't know how the game ended until you just sort of gave the very brief spark notes version.

01:12:25.020 --> 01:12:39.360
<v Sean>But that I will grant to Joe's point, any story that actually has some sort of self-contained narrative and isn't like sound like a five year old child made up the aliens that you're shooting in a shmup.

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:42.040
<v Sean>Like, I'm a huge fan of that.

01:12:42.640 --> 01:12:46.400
<v Sean>And I think that is that puts it in the upper echelon of stories on the NES.

01:12:47.000 --> 01:12:55.860
<v Sean>But granted, when you said like the basic like key points there, you kind of get that vibe in the bar.

01:12:55.880 --> 01:12:58.160
<v Sean>Like you don't even need to leave the bar.

01:12:58.160 --> 01:13:04.400
<v Sean>And you're already kind of like, oh, some doctor injected me with something because there's all this shit on the on the counter.

01:13:04.740 --> 01:13:10.660
<v Sean>And I I work above a doctor and they keep mentioning the seagull guy.

01:13:10.800 --> 01:13:13.560
<v Sean>There's a there's a very large lady in the trunk of this car.

01:13:13.840 --> 01:13:15.260
<v Joe>This all makes sense.

01:13:16.140 --> 01:13:27.120
<v Mike>Yeah. And Sean, just to be clear, that's kind of the story beats I gave were just like the the entirety of the setup of the the amnesia element.

01:13:27.120 --> 01:13:37.100
<v Mike>And the final like win state is actually pretty interesting because then once you piece it all together, you have to go to the police and prove your innocence.

01:13:37.560 --> 01:13:42.180
<v Mike>And you you know, you're basically like tracking down all the correct evidence.

01:13:42.180 --> 01:13:47.220
<v Mike>But furthermore, you have to get rid of the bad evidence, the framing stuff.

01:13:47.420 --> 01:13:50.800
<v Mike>Like if you go into the police, that's illegal, Mike.

01:13:51.260 --> 01:13:52.220
<v Mike>But I'm saying, yeah, exactly.

01:13:52.340 --> 01:13:58.720
<v Mike>That's what's so weird, though, is like if you go into the police station, the game, the game assumes that you handed over everything.

01:13:58.860 --> 01:14:03.660
<v Mike>And so then they find the other stuff and they're like, all right, well, you're you know, you clearly did it.

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:12.760
<v Mike>And so it's like you have to not only find everything that would prove that the mafia was behind it, you have to throw out the bad evidence.

01:14:13.380 --> 01:14:14.540
<v Mike>That is funny.

01:14:14.680 --> 01:14:23.800
<v Sean>And I did when I went onto the street the first time and saw the police station, I had already seen, you know, a lot of evidence for wrongdoing.

01:14:25.120 --> 01:14:26.680
<v Sean>I guess you always would.

01:14:26.920 --> 01:14:35.760
<v Sean>But I guess there is some there is some version of this game where you just sort of find your way outside and beeline to the police and have no idea why they've arrested you.

01:14:36.240 --> 01:14:47.220
<v Mike>Right. Is the is the jump from the bar to the sewers like to drastic?

01:14:47.720 --> 01:14:52.200
<v Mike>Like, why are we traveling by sewer like so early in the game?

01:14:52.340 --> 01:14:59.280
<v Mike>You know what I mean? Like, could it have been more like could it have gotten more extreme as time went on?

01:14:59.480 --> 01:15:08.140
<v Mike>It's kind of weird that you can just go like straight from waking up in this bathroom to like and he chose the sewers to travel around.

01:15:08.460 --> 01:15:11.820
<v Joe>Yeah. But I guess it depends on on your playthrough.

01:15:11.920 --> 01:15:18.140
<v Joe>I feel like it wasn't quite as quite so early for me that I found the sewer because I kind of had explored a lot before.

01:15:18.140 --> 01:15:25.140
<v Joe>You know, I like and I was, you know, outside seeing the manhole cover and I was like, well, I wonder what's down there.

01:15:26.660 --> 01:15:28.400
<v Joe>So, like, I feel like it didn't happen right away.

01:15:28.560 --> 01:15:35.680
<v Joe>And also, it's like if I'm like, I get that I'm trying to solve some mystery.

01:15:35.680 --> 01:15:44.480
<v Joe>So, like, I don't know, it's not it wasn't that crazy to me to be like, oh, I wonder if I can, like, you know, get into an unlocked area through the sewer or something.

01:15:44.540 --> 01:15:50.460
<v Joe>But I get what you're saying that it's not like it may be like it has a totally different vibe than the rest of the area.

01:15:50.640 --> 01:15:52.020
<v Mike>Well, Joe, that's a great point.

01:15:52.020 --> 01:15:57.900
<v Mike>I liked it is a great point because that's not how like I didn't open up a sewer.

01:15:58.260 --> 01:16:02.880
<v Mike>I didn't open up a manhole to go down into the sewer because I agree that would be a bit of a stretch.

01:16:03.300 --> 01:16:16.060
<v Mike>I opened the curtains of where the dead guy was, saw the fire escape, went upwards to the third floor, got on the elevator and rode the elevator down to the casino.

01:16:16.420 --> 01:16:17.240
<v Mike>And then. Yeah.

01:16:17.600 --> 01:16:19.160
<v Joe>And that takes it. See, I went the opposite.

01:16:19.260 --> 01:16:29.240
<v Sean>I went for that. I never I got to the casino by going outside, going into the sewer, exploring, and ended up in the casino, which took me to the elevator, which took me back to that room where that guy was.

01:16:29.340 --> 01:16:32.800
<v Joe>And I was like, OK, now I understand there's an elevator that will take me through this this building.

01:16:33.340 --> 01:16:36.200
<v Joe>So, like, it wasn't like, oh, I just went for this building, the sewer somehow.

01:16:36.460 --> 01:16:39.540
<v Joe>It was like, no, I went outside and deliberately crawled into the sewer.

01:16:39.540 --> 01:17:04.640
<v Sean>Just for completeness and balance, I guess I'll say that I got into the sewer by going down into the basement from the main room of the bar, hitting the the loose book on the bookcase, having the bookcase move out of the way, going into that central room, finding the finding the whole casino and elevator situation.

01:17:04.640 --> 01:17:08.100
<v Sean>But also seeing the ladder down into the sewer.

01:17:08.660 --> 01:17:11.840
<v Sean>But all this that we've said of how he got to the sewer.

01:17:12.080 --> 01:17:13.700
<v Joe>Do you ever need to go into the sewer?

01:17:14.260 --> 01:17:15.320
<v Mike>I great question.

01:17:16.420 --> 01:17:17.540
<v Sean>Yes, you do.

01:17:18.660 --> 01:17:26.420
<v Joe>Well, as far as I know, unless unless the toilet, which I didn't know about, is another way to do it later in the game.

01:17:26.420 --> 01:17:28.360
<v Joe>Towards the very end, you need to get rid of evidence.

01:17:28.720 --> 01:17:33.820
<v Joe>I see. And there's like that big deep water area that you can throw all your evidence into.

01:17:33.820 --> 01:17:35.440
<v Joe>I guess that's the only way to get rid of things.

01:17:36.940 --> 01:17:39.200
<v Joe>But I love that we all found it in different ways.

01:17:39.300 --> 01:17:40.920
<v Joe>That's actually really, really cool.

01:17:41.040 --> 01:17:45.200
<v Joe>And I also I didn't know anything about the book until I watched a video after I had played it.

01:17:46.240 --> 01:17:47.940
<v Mike>Yeah, I thought you were going to talk about the wine.

01:17:48.840 --> 01:17:50.340
<v Mike>Yeah. Sorry, you're right.

01:17:50.460 --> 01:17:51.520
<v Sean>Wine bottle. Oh, the wine bottle.

01:17:51.960 --> 01:17:54.600
<v Joe>Did you select that because there was no dust on it?

01:17:54.600 --> 01:18:00.180
<v Sean>I examined and there was it was weird that it said that the one on the far right has no dust on it.

01:18:00.360 --> 01:18:07.100
<v Sean>So I just I just hit both of the ones on the right and the one actually caused the door to open.

01:18:07.340 --> 01:18:10.080
<v Sean>So I yeah, thought that was pretty cool.

